      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bridgman%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A railroad station opened the same year, anchoring the town’s early growth around the tracks.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bridgman%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The village was officially renamed Bridgman on April 9, 1874, after the local bridge builder George C. Bridgman.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bridgman%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Bridgman expanded by annexing the former Charlotteville area, uniting the two communities into one city.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bridgman%2C_Michigan",
